# XSH atomic_store(), atomic_store_explicit()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# ISO C interface. The stored value is observed with a plain read of the
# atomic object (itself an atomic load), not with atomic_load().

assertion  XSH.atomic_store.001
anchor     functions/atomic_store.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2024
says       atomic_store() replaces the value of an atomic object, for
           atomic_bool and every atomic integer type, with the values 0, 1,
           the type's minimum and its maximum.
test       t/001-store-values.c

assertion  XSH.atomic_store.002
anchor     functions/atomic_store.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2024
says       atomic_store_explicit() with memory_order_relaxed, _release and
           _seq_cst (the orders allowed for a store) replaces the value, for
           every atomic integer type.
test       t/002-store-explicit-orders.c

assertion  XSH.atomic_store.003
anchor     functions/atomic_store.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2024
says       atomic_store() and atomic_store_explicit() replace the pointer
           held by atomic pointer objects (int, structure, function
           pointers, a null pointer).
test       t/003-store-pointer.c

assertion  XSH.atomic_store.004
anchor     functions/atomic_store.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2024
says       A store changes only the object it names: in an array of five
           atomic objects of each type, storing to one element leaves the
           others unchanged, with all-ones-like and all-zeros-like
           neighbours.
test       t/004-store-isolated.c

assertion  XSH.atomic_store.005
anchor     functions/atomic_store.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap04.html#4.15.1
class      required
since      2024
says       Ordinary data written before atomic_store_explicit(release) (or
           before atomic_store()) is visible to a thread whose
           atomic_load_explicit(acquire) (or atomic_load()) returns the
           stored value, in 20000 messages, for both spellings.
note       The wait for the last message is bounded (20 s); not seeing it is
           UNRESOLVED, since the page does not bound the delay.
test       t/005-store-release-acquire.c
